In AWS EC2, metadata and user data are two different things.

1. Metadata = Information about the EC2 instance
Metadata of EC2 means some information you might being able to check about your instance ID. You are just provided with key pair and IP address.
You don't have console access, only server access. So how will you check the public or private IP of your instance? We can do curl.
Metadata is information AWS provides about the instance itself.

169.254.169.254 is NOT a global/public IP.
It is a link-local IPv4 address reserved for accessing the EC2 Instance Metadata Service (IMDS).
Why do every EC2 instances use the same IP?

Because 169.254.169.254 is a special metadata endpoint. It doesn't identify a particular EC2 instance.

For example:
EC2-A → 169.254.169.254 → EC2-A's metadata
EC2-B → 169.254.169.254 → EC2-B's metadata
The same address gives different information depending on which EC2 instance is making the request.
So remember:
169.254.169.254 = special link-local metadata address
It is not your EC2's public/private IP address.


curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id

For private IP:
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/local-ipv4

For public IP:
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/public-ipv4

Examples:
Instance ID
Private IP address
Public IP address
Availability Zone
IAM role information
Security credentials information
Hostname

You can access it from inside the EC2 instance through the Instance Metadata Service.

Example:

curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/

Think:

Metadata = "Tell me information about this EC2."



