IMDS means Instance Metadata Service.

It is an AWS service running at a special IP address:

169.254.169.254

It allows an EC2 instance to ask AWS for information about itself.

Example:

EC2
 ↓
IMDS
 ↓
"My Instance ID?"
 ↓
i-123456789

It can provide things like:

Instance ID
Private IP
Availability Zone
IAM role credentials
Instance type

Simple meaning:

IMDS = a service inside AWS that lets an EC2 instance get its own metadata.




If you mean “Is 169.254.169.254 permanent?” — yes, the IMDS address is a fixed link-local address used to access EC2 instance metadata.

169.254.169.254

You don't change it for different EC2 instances.

For example, inside any EC2 instance:

curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/

The address stays the same, but the information returned is different for each EC2 instance.

Example:

EC2-A → 169.254.169.254 → EC2-A's metadata
EC2-B → 169.254.169.254 → EC2-B's metadata

So:

IP address = fixed
Metadata returned = specific to that EC2


2. User Data = Instructions/script you give to EC2

User data is something you provide when launching the EC2.

For example:

#!/bin/bash
yum install -y httpd
systemctl start httpd

When the EC2 starts, this script can automatically install and configure software.

Think:

User data = "When you start, do these things."

Simple example

You launch an EC2:

User Data:

#!/bin/bash
yum install -y httpd
systemctl start httpd

EC2 starts → reads the user data → installs Apache → starts Apache.


Yes, you can use sudo with user-data commands, but usually you don't need to.

Why?

EC2 User Data scripts normally run as the root user.

So this:

#!/bin/bash
yum install -y httpd
systemctl start httpd

works directly.

You don't need:

sudo yum install -y httpd
sudo systemctl start httpd
But if you manually run commands after logging in

You are usually a normal user, so you may need sudo:

sudo yum install -y httpd
sudo systemctl start httpd

Remember:

User Data → runs as root → usually no sudo needed.
SSH login → normal user → sudo may be needed.








